We currently call the following:


{code:php}$resolver = \core\di::get(\Invoker\CallableResolver::class);
$callable = $resolver->resolve($callable);{code}

The resolver does not just resolve a string, but resolves a callable “into a real PHP callable”. What this actually means is that, under the hook, it calls:

{noformat}        if (is_array($callable) && is_string($callable[0])) {
            try {
                // Replace the container entry name by the actual object
                $callable[0] = $this->container->get($callable[0]);
                return $callable;{noformat}

This causes it to be instantiated.

In our case we know that the callable passed is, in 99% of cases, the actual class name, for example:

{noformat}\core\router\util::get_path_for_callable([\core_user\route\api\preferences::class, 'get_preferences'], ['user' => 1);{noformat}

We can easily short-circuit this by doing something like:


{noformat}if (is_string($callable) && strpos($callable, '::') !== false) {
    $callable = explode('::', $callable, 2);
}

if (is_array($callable) && count($callable) === 2) {
    if (class_exists($callable[0]) && method_exists($callable[0], $callable[1])) {
        return $callable[0] . '::' . $callable[1];
    }
}{noformat}



This will still defer to the current approach if the class does not exist, or the method on the class does not exist.

In addition to CI testing on our Jenkins Infra, we will also want to add appropriate testing on GHA for Moodle installed via Composer.

There are a few ways to do this, but the easiest is probably to do something like:

{noformat}composer create-project moodle/seed moodle
cd moodle
composer repository add moodle git ${{ github.repository }}
composer config minimum-stability dev
composer require moodle/moodle:dev-${{ github.sha }}}
composer require moodle/moodle-testing
// Create the config.php
// Install Moodle as usual
// Test away...{noformat}

